  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 8+8-2 & =0 \\\\ 14 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is non-invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
